.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
A retained physical relationship between any two \fBPhysicsBody\fP owners\&. 


.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
\fBAPI Stability: 2 — Evolving\fP\&. This API is implemented and supported, but its contract is still settling\&. Compatibility is preserved where practical; breaking changes are documented in release notes\&. See \fBAPI stability\fP\&.
.RE
.PP
Create through \fBPhysicsBody\fP methods, such as createPinJoint(), createSpring() or createMotor()\&. The same API joins Sprite-to-Sprite, Sprite-to-Part and Part-to-Part\&. Shapes are optional\&. A constraint never changes a \fBPart\fP's parent or bone binding\&. Do not construct this class directly\&.

.PP
The factory receiver is body A; the \fRother\fP argument is B\&. Anchor coordinates are local to the physical body, without artwork scaling\&. Relative angles and angular speeds mean B minus A, clockwise positive, in radians and radians/second\&. Motors and forces act on physical state; they do not schedule \fBAnimated\fP operations\&.

.PP
Bodies retain their active constraints\&. Removing a body or removing/transferring an owner from its layer disconnects them\&. Retained JavaScript handles remain safe and return \fBPhysicsBody::NoPhysics\fP for both endpoints\&. A retained native handle requires addRef()/release()\&. Constraints created before insertion stay dormant until both bodies share a world\&. Joining two different layer worlds throws\&.

.PP
Basic uses bounded impulse approximations; Chipmunk uses native constraints\&. Neither promises exact preservation under arbitrary loads or large timesteps\&. Both support force/torque caps, break thresholds and connected-body collision suppression\&. No response is applied when neither endpoint is dynamic\&.

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
Instantiate both PhysicsBodies first\&. Owner updates advance constraints; calling \fBPhysicsBody::step()\fP on one detached body does not advance the other\&. Native custom worlds can call PhysicsBody::solveConstraints() with both endpoints\&.
.RE
.PP
.PP
.nf
const mount = sprite\&.createPart('mount');
const pendant = sprite\&.createPart('pendant');
mount\&.setupPhysicsBody()\&.setMode(pdg\&.physicsBody_Static);
pendant\&.setLocation(0, 30);
pendant\&.setupPhysicsBody(1, 20);
const tether = mount\&.physics\&.createSpring(pendant\&.physics,
    new pdg\&.Point(0, 0), new pdg\&.Point(0, 0), 30, 20, 5);
tether\&.setMaxForce(100)\&.setBreakForce(90);
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBCollider\fP, \fBPhysicsBody\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "disconnect ()"

.PP
Disconnect this constraint safely\&. Idempotent\&. Both endpoint lists drop it; a retained handle remains usable and reports NoPhysics endpoints\&. No implicit reconnection occurs\&. 
.SS "\fBPoint\fP getAnchorA ()"

.PP
Read the connection point on body A\&. Pin, slide, pivot and spring constraints support this getter\&. For a groove, use \fBgetGrooveStart()\fP and \fBgetGrooveEnd()\fP instead\&. Other constraint types throw\&. Coordinates are unscaled body-local distance units, relative to \fBgetBodyA()\fP's physical frame, not a bone's joint origin or the \fBSprite\fP's frame\&. A retained disconnected constraint stays queryable\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A copied \fBPoint\fP; changing it does not change the constraint\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBsetAnchorA()\fP, \fBsetAnchors()\fP 
.RE
.PP

.SS "\fBPoint\fP getAnchorB ()"

.PP
Read the connection point on body B\&. Pin, slide, pivot, groove and spring constraints support this getter\&. Other constraint types throw\&. Coordinates are unscaled body-local distance units, relative to \fBgetBodyB()\fP's physical frame, not a bone's joint origin or the \fBSprite\fP's frame\&. A retained disconnected constraint stays queryable\&.

.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
A copied \fBPoint\fP; changing it does not change the constraint\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBSee also\fP
.RS 4
\fBsetAnchorB()\fP, \fBsetAnchors()\fP 
.RE
.PP

